\details{
\strong{Model specification.} The expected count model is a Poisson regression
with:
\itemize{
\item Log person-time offset
\item BYM2 spatial random effects (Riebler et al. 2016)
\item Optional seasonal component
\item Optional overdispersion (iid or negative binomial)
\item Optional covariates
}

\strong{Input validation.} Executed before INLA runs:
\enumerate{
\item Required columns present and correct types
\item No negative counts or NAs
\item All district ids in cases appear in adjacency
\item No duplicate district x month combinations
\item Population coverage check
\item Adjacency graph connectivity check
\item Covariate validation
}

\strong{Computational notes.}
\itemize{
\item Posterior sampling can be time-consuming for large datasets
\item Use fewer draws for initial exploration
\item INLA uses sparse matrix methods for efficiency
}

\strong{Interpreting INLA's diagnostic chatter (\code{debug = TRUE}).}
\itemize{
\item \verb{vb.correction aborted} / \verb{iterative process seems to diverge} --
INLA's variational Bayes correction is an \emph{optional} refinement on
top of the Laplace approximation. When it fails to converge, INLA
returns the Laplace result, which is a valid (slightly less
accurate) posterior. This message is informational, not an error.
\item \verb{Matrix is not positive definite} \emph{during fitting} -- usually means
the BYM2 precision matrix is singular; check that the adjacency
graph is symmetric and that disconnected components are handled
(see \code{\link[=spi_adjacency]{spi_adjacency()}}).
}

\strong{CPO / PIT and \code{overdispersion = "iid"}.} When the model has an iid
effect per observation, conditional predictive ordinate (CPO) and the
probability integral transform (PIT) are structurally unreliable --
INLA flags most observations as \code{failure = 1}. This is an artifact of
the model spec, not a sign of poor fit. For CPO-based diagnostics, use
the default \code{overdispersion = "nb"} or \code{overdispersion = "none"}.
}
\section{Paper specification}{

The call the paper uses is \code{season = "harmonic"}, \code{year_effect = "iid"},
\code{overdispersion = "nb"}, with no covariates -- the defaults below. The
published index, \code{spi_prospective()}, is built on a fit with
these defaults.
}

\section{Choosing a seasonal specification}{

Surveillance counts often show within-year cycles driven by transmission
biology, climate, school terms, or reporting patterns. Picking the wrong
\code{season} term either under-fits (genuine cycles bleed into the spatial term
and bias risk estimates) or over-fits (noisy month-of-year effects steal
signal from the expected count).

\strong{Quick diagnostic.} Before fitting, plot the monthly average count to see
whether a cycle exists and what shape it has:

\preformatted{
cases |>
  dplyr::mutate(m = lubridate::month(month)) |>
  dplyr::group_by(m) |>
  dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(count, na.rm = TRUE)) |>
  plot(type = "b")
}

\strong{How to choose:}
\itemize{
\item \emph{"none"} -- no visible cycle, or counts look flat across months.
Use for aseasonal outcomes (e.g. neonatal tetanus, DHIS2 reporting
completeness when administratively driven).
\item \emph{"harmonic"} (default, 4 terms: sin/cos at 12 and 6 month periods)
-- one or two smooth peaks per year. Good for most VPDs. Most
parsimonious option that still captures seasonality.
\item \emph{"rw2"} -- cyclic 2nd-order random walk over 12 months. Smooth but
arbitrary shape; lets the prior do the smoothing rather than imposing a
sinusoid. Use when seasonality is real but asymmetric or multi-modal
(e.g. cholera in some settings).
\item \emph{"monthly"} -- 11 free monthly fixed effects (January as reference).
Most flexible, no smoothing. Use only with plentiful data (~3+ years
across most districts); otherwise monthly effects absorb noise.
}

\strong{If unsure, compare.} Fit two specifications and pick the lower DIC /
WAIC (printed in the verbose run). Differences > ~5 on either criterion are
meaningful; smaller is noise.

\strong{Disease-specific starting points} (acute case-based surveillance):
\tabular{ll}{
AFP / polio                  \tab harmonic        \cr
Measles                      \tab harmonic or rw2 \cr
Meningitis (African belt)    \tab harmonic        \cr
Yellow fever                 \tab harmonic        \cr
Cholera                      \tab rw2 or monthly  \cr
Neonatal tetanus             \tab none            \cr
DHIS2 reporting completeness \tab none or rw2     \cr
}

These are starting points, not prescriptions -- always verify against the
monthly-average plot for your data and country, since seasonality varies by
climate zone and surveillance system.
}

\section{Reproducibility}{

Three separate things have to be pinned before two runs of the same code
agree, and \code{seed} on its own covers only one of them:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Which posterior configuration each draw comes from.}
\code{inla.posterior.sample()} picks this with R's own RNG, so \code{set.seed()}
governs it.
\item \strong{The latent field drawn within that configuration.} This comes from an
RNG inside INLA's compiled code that R's \code{set.seed()} cannot reach, so
the seed has to be handed to INLA directly.
\item \strong{The fit itself.} \code{INLA::inla()} is not bit-reproducible when it runs
multithreaded: the hyperparameter mode and integration points shift with
thread scheduling, so the draws are taken from a slightly different
posterior. \code{num_threads} governs this.
}

\code{spi_expected()} handles (1) and (2) whenever \code{seed} is non-NULL, restoring
the caller's RNG state afterwards. (3) is why \code{num_threads} defaults to
\code{"1:1"} alongside a seed.

Leaving any one of them loose does not merely perturb the last decimal: it
moves SPI values and flips field-guide verdicts, and it can do so for a
district whose own numbers barely moved, because corroboration reads
\emph{neighbouring} districts' posteriors, which drift too.

The defaults are therefore reproducible but serial, which costs wall clock
against a multithreaded fit. For exploratory work where determinism does
not matter, pass \code{num_threads = NULL} to inherit INLA's global thread
setting, or \code{seed = NULL} to opt out entirely.

One caveat on how far this goes: the guarantee is agreement to numerical
tolerance, not bit-identity. INLA's mode-finding is not bit-stable even
pinned to one thread, so two seeded fits of the same data can still differ
in the sixth significant figure, measured at up to \code{1.6e-6} relative. That
is orders of magnitude smaller than anything that could move an SPI
classification, and four orders below the drift described above, which a
loose seed makes large enough to change a verdict.
}

